| **`B-Edge-Multi-Artist-Salon-BRD-v1.md`** | **New, Sep 21, 2026.** Business requirements for putting more than one artist in a salon — the capability Rania asked for. Opens with the finding that reframes the work: **the schema has been salon-shaped since migration 001 (ten tables carry `salon_id`, `artists.salon_id` is nullable) and the application has never been.** Names six verified gaps: **G1** `salons.owner_id` is never consulted for any authorisation decision anywhere in the codebase, so every member could edit the owner's prices, stores and payment methods; **G2** the only `INSERT INTO salons` is inside `onboarding/repository.go:63`, a transaction that creates salon+artist+store together, so a salon can never gain a second member; **G3** `business_hours` is **store-scoped**, so two artists at one store are forced to share one schedule — found while writing this document, and arguably larger than G1; **G4** billing is per artist and `plans.seat_price`/`included_seats` are populated for all six plans but enter **no calculation**; **G5** `salon_payment_methods` has no `artist_id` while earnings filter `WHERE artist_id`; **G6** no customer-facing artist choice. Carries 13 business rules, 40 numbered requirements, a Mermaid use-case diagram with `«include»`/`«extend»` edges, and eleven D-MS decisions with recommendations — three flagged **FOUNDER** because they change revenue, privacy posture or fairness to members. |
| **`B-Edge-Multi-Artist-Salon-HLD-v1.md`** | **New, Sep 21, 2026.** High level design. Thesis: *the schema is already salon-shaped, the application is soloist-shaped — this is authorisation and scheduling, not re-architecture.* Introduces **`internal/pkg/salonrole`**, a leaf package holding a 17-capability × 2-role matrix as the single source of truth, explicitly because `subscriptionVisibleCond` existed as three hand-written copies and two were still wrong a day after the third was fixed. Role is **derived** from `salons.owner_id` and resolved at token issue, never stored — with the consequence written down, that ownership transfer must invalidate both parties' tokens. Contains the middleware chain, the invitation state machine, two sequence diagrams and a deployment/implementation view showing that this feature **adds no process, no external dependency and no scheduler**. Seven risks, R1 being that the capability check gets re-expressed inline — the project's signature defect. |
| **`B-Edge-Multi-Artist-Salon-LLD-v1.md`** | **New, Sep 21, 2026.** Low level design and UML. Package layout, a Mermaid class diagram, the full `salonrole` source with its matrix, and `internal/pkg/schedule` — pure interval algebra with the load-bearing default that **a missing rota row means "available for the whole store window", not "unavailable"**, which is what makes the change invisible to the five soloists on the platform. Specifies ten API contracts with their exact failure codes, notes that `GET /invitations/:token` is a public enumeration surface whose unknown/expired/revoked responses must be indistinguishable, and orders the `Invite` steps so phone normalisation precedes any uniqueness check and notification queuing sits **outside** the transaction (61 of 61 notifications ever queued are `dead`). Adds two anti-drift tests: matrix completeness, and `TestNoInlineOwnerChecks`, which parses `internal/` and fails on any `owner_id` comparison outside the two files allowed to make one. |
